Z||000000||PTW87GT||12-04-2000||08:49 AM||||Has anyone changed the coolant lines on the V6?  I have one that is going bad.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||btoth||12-04-2000||09:01 AM||||which coolent lines?  under the car or up front or in the engine compartment?||||reg||1
Z||000002||PTW87GT||12-04-2000||12:15 PM||||The long lind that run along the floor pan.  The curve up and around the suspension.  The are stainless steel pipe.  I am thinking of making new out of copper.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||12-04-2000||12:42 PM||||I have replaced the drivers side cooling pipe in my 85 SE.  It was a lot harder than the Haynes manual let on.  To remove the drivers side pipe you need to remove the steering rack for the pipe to clear the frame when you swing it out.  <P>The passenger side pipe comes out much easier I think.  With the rack removed I went ahead a rebuilt the front suspension in my SE.  <P>I think you would have to support copper lines more often since copper is a much softer material.  You should be able to get good used pipes from Ed PArks or Kick Hill Farms.  Rodney Dickman might sell new replacement pipes.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||12-04-2000||01:39 PM||||My driver side one leaked bad when I bought the car.  GM doesn't sell replacements anymore but the make a kit that can be used to patch them, so if it only has a few bad spots any GM place should be able to repair it for you.  I forget how much mine cost though.||||reg||1
